WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.430
Film Courage:&nbsp; What are the top five screenwriters
that someone should study?

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:21.110
Karl Iglesias:&nbsp; Wow…well if you go to the
101 Habits&nbsp; [Editor’s Note - Karl Iglesias’s

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:27.110
book The 101 Habits of Highly Successful Screenwriters]
there are 14 of the top writers I recommend.&nbsp;

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:32.910
You can see their work and the new version
(10th anniversary edition) had 18 new writers

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:39.170
like Tony Gilroy and Terry Rossio, all the
big names.&nbsp;

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:46.050
Between the two, you have many writers, your
mentors and their advice to you.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:56.699
In terms of writers I admire, Pixar, Pixar
storytellers, Brad Bird, John Lasseter.&nbsp;

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:01.370
The thing with John Lasseter that is really
interesting is…just to show you the power

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.150
that he had as a storyteller.&nbsp;

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:09.980
When Disney bought Pixar and John Lasseter
became the head of animation, all of the Disney

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:14.090
animation movies have been successful, like
really great storytelling starting with BOLT,

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:18.159
all the way to TANGLED, FROZEN, to the latest
one UTOPIA.&nbsp;

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:22.430
So these are not Pixar movies but you can
see the PIXAR touch in them and they have

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:23.840
been successful with audiences.&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.880
So it kind of shows you that he’s had quite
and influence at Disney.&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:36.180
So yeah, I admire him, and hundreds and hundreds
of other writers.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:43.530
Film Courage:&nbsp; When you were in High School
or College, what were some of your favorite

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.750
screenplays where you thought “I’ve just
got to do something like this!”

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.000
Karl Iglesias:&nbsp; Well for me it was mostly
genre.&nbsp;

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:59.369
I’ve always been a sci-fi person, specifically
futuristic as opposed to sci-fi (I like to

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:01.780
make that distinction).&nbsp;

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:08.869
Futuristic is more about in the future but
on Earth, as opposed to Star Trek, Star Wars,

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:10.040
you know space ships in space.&nbsp;

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:20.030
I tend to gravitate toward more thought-provoking
stories like BLADE RUNNER, THE MATRIX, INCEPTION,

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.790
anything that has a really thought-provoking
story and concept taking place on Earth in

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:25.790
the future.&nbsp;

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:33.519
ETERNAL SUNSHINE OF THE SPOTLESS MIND is a
favorite of mine, so any thought-provoking

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:34.950
movie I tend to like.&nbsp;

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.560
Film Noir, futuristic, thrillers.&nbsp;

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:43.950
I still remember the day I saw SILENCE OF
THE LAMBS, an advanced screening of it.&nbsp;

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:52.010
And it was the very first movie where I totally
was not aware of anything else.&nbsp;

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.120
I remember the lights went down and you watched
the movie and the next thing I knew, the lights

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.120
coming on.&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:58.120
It was like “Oh, my God.&nbsp;

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.470
Two hours went by and I was completely mesmerized
from beginning to end, every second.&nbsp;

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.200
That has never happened before in a movie.&nbsp;

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:10.220
That was the first time I remember being so
taken and engaged by a movie.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.750
Film Courage:&nbsp; What do you think it was that
made you taken with it?

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.319
Karl Iglesias: &nbsp; Just great craftsmanship.&nbsp;

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:17.780
It was just amazing.&nbsp;

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:18.780
Everything about it.&nbsp;

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:26.420
It was an interesting story, compelling characters,
great relationships, fantastic twists and

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:32.840
cleverness, caring about not only the main
character but caring about the villain.&nbsp;

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:33.959
Just great craftsmanship.&nbsp;

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:38.739
Kudos to Thomas Harris who wrote the book,
it was very close to the movie.&nbsp;

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:43.990
And to Ted Tally who wrote the screenplay
and Jonathan Demme who directed it.&nbsp;

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:49.079
I mean this was one of the very few movies
that swept the top five Oscars.&nbsp;

00:03:49.079 --> 00:03:54.420
You have best movie, best director, best screenplay,
best actor, best actress.&nbsp;

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:57.930
There haven’t been that many movies in the
history of movies that have received the best

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:58.930
five.&nbsp;

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.829
It’s considered the pantheon of movies.&nbsp;

00:04:00.829 --> 00:04:07.700
I remember the first one was IT HAPPENED ONE
NIGHT with Clark Gable and Claudette Colbert,

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.930
that was another favorite of mine.&nbsp;

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:16.930
I believe ONE FLEW OVER THE CUCKOO’S NEST
also won all top five awards if I’m not

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:17.930
mistaken?&nbsp;

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:20.910
But I think SILENCE OF THE LAMBS WAS THE LAST
ONE.&nbsp;

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:29.150
I’ll have to research that but I remember
when I saw it thinking ‘Oh my, God.&nbsp;

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:33.990
If anyone else has this same experience it’s
going to be great, a classic, it will sweep

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.830
the Oscars, I mean everything.&nbsp;

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:38.210
And I was rooting for that film.&nbsp;

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:43.430
I remember watching the Oscars when that happened
and just being like “Wow!”&nbsp;

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.650
It’s one of my all time favorites.&nbsp;

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:49.250
And then BLADE RUNNER is one of my top favorites.&nbsp;

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:54.810
Although that didn’t do as well when it
opened and it was actually considered a flop

00:04:54.810 --> 00:05:02.371
and only became a classic through word of
mouth and the sheer visual of the story.

00:05:02.371 --> 00:05:04.872
Film Courage:&nbsp; It’s an interesting concept
when something doesn’t show the [box office]

00:05:04.872 --> 00:05:06.919
numbers but then this cult audience finds
it…

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:07.919
Karl Iglesias:&nbsp; Yes.&nbsp;

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:09.090
There are quite a few movies like this.&nbsp;

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.520
SHAWSHENK REDEMPTION is another one.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:17.280
It kind of flopped when it opened at the box
office but then created a life of its own

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:18.750
from the sheer audience.&nbsp;

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.550
It’s like “Wow!&nbsp;

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:21.710
They responded to it.”

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:24.150
Film Courage:&nbsp; What is that about?

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:28.310
Karl Iglesias:&nbsp; Mostly I think it is about
concept.&nbsp;

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:31.860
I mean when you look at the concept for SHAWSHENK
REDEMPTION there is nothing appealing about

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:33.110
it, right?&nbsp;

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:36.460
It’s a movie about two people in prison.&nbsp;

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:37.660
Okay?&nbsp;

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:43.460
But when you experience it….And this is
a lesson for writers in that just because

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:48.669
you don’t have a high concept….now with
a beginning writer a high concept is paramount.

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:53.310
&nbsp; It helps you because it helps you break
in because your script will be requested and

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:56.490
then once they get the script in front of
them and it’s well executed then you’re

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.100
on your way in.&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:03.310
So I don’t recommend having low concepts
when you’re a beginner, but in this case….I

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:11.430
mean it was Frank Darabont, Stephen King’s
novella, but it was a low concept.&nbsp;

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:15.020
There was nothing that made it appealing,
so it didn’t really drive audiences to the

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:19.610
theaters at the time but the execution is
masterful.&nbsp;

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:25.472
So once you’re in the door and you experience
it, you’re like “Oh, my God!” and then

00:06:25.472 --> 00:06:27.280
you tell others and word of mouth.&nbsp;

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:28.949
That is how it happens.&nbsp;

00:06:28.949 --> 00:06:30.690
So that is the case with that.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:31.690
QUESTION FOR THE VIEWERS:&nbsp; Can you name a
movie that has won all 5 major awards at the

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:31.691
Oscars?

